{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Fjord Ice Melt: Psychrophile Wake-Up in Kongsfjorden",
    "date": "2026-08-17",
    "lesson": "Each Arctic spring, meltwater pulses off glacier fronts in Svalbard's Kongsfjorden inject a thin, cold, brackish lens over denser seawater. Sediment and organic debris scoured from the glacier bed ride this lens, and the psychrophilic (cold-loving) bacteria that overwintered as cysts in the dark must wake in water still near 0C, where every enzyme reaction runs at a crawl (Q10 penalty in full force). Diatoms trapped in the meltwater and sea-ice underside seed the base of the bloom, but the window to feed before winter darkness cost catches up is short and food is patchy in the cold, sluggish, sediment-laden water. This models the trade-off psychrophiles must make: divert scarce energy into cold-adapted (often lipid-rich, cryoprotective) enzymes versus staying dormant longer.",
    "citation": "Kongsfjorden glacier-meltwater plume studies; Arctic sea-ice/glacier microbial ecology (e.g. Institute Polaire studies, Svalbard)",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Kongsfjorden, Svalbard: glacial meltwater plume microbial communities in early Arctic spring"
  },
